	(text "Boot/EEPROM_EN/PMIC_EN"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 154.178 50.8 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 2.032 2.032)
			)
		)
		(uuid "807e8285-9c1a-47ad-8cd7-d74550796211")
	)
	(text "POWER-CYCLE CONTROL (nPWR_CYCLE)\nLets the CM5 cut its own power after a clean shutdown and restart\nby itself. Use it when a normal reboot is not enough: it fully resets\nthe PMIC and all on-module power rails.\n\nHow it works:\n- GPIO6 drives U9 (74LVC1G07, open-drain). J5 pins 5-6 connect U9's\n  output to PMIC_EN (CM5 pin 99, 100k internal pull-up to 5V).\n- GPIO6 high (default pull-up at reset): U9 output is off and the\n  CM5 runs.\n- GPIO6 low: PMIC_EN is pulled low and the CM5 powers off.\n- U9 is powered from 3V3_CM5, which switches off with the CM5, so\n  U9 releases PMIC_EN and the CM5 powers back on.\n\nUse:\n- Fit the J5 5-6 jumper to enable. Fit J5 5-6 only after the \n  board and GPIO6 behaviour have been verified.\n- config.txt: dtoverlay=gpio-poweroff,gpiopin=6,active_low=1\n  \"sudo poweroff\" then shuts down cleanly and power-cycles the CM5.\n- Do not drive GPIO6 low while the OS is running. CM5 datasheet\n  2.12.2: pull PMIC_EN low only after the OS has shut down.\n- The low time here is set by how fast 3V3_CM5 decays and is not\n  guaranteed. This circuit is carried over from the CM4 board and is\n  not yet proven on a CM5: check it with a scope on first boards.\n"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 146.812 98.552 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.016 1.016)
			)
			(justify left)
		)
		(uuid "89b36b0b-04e7-4b46-a8b2-e1bcf5587b40")
	)
	(text "GPIO8"
		(exclude_from_sim no)
		(at 109.982 73.914 0)
		(effects
			(font
				(size 1.27 1.27)
			)
		)
		(uuid "adf0df37-0cc5-491f-bb77-fa9dbc09b682")
	)
